      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The scanner correlates a network primitive with a dynamic-construction sink, but the actual data flows are benign and proportionate to the stated purpose. The fetch calls are same-origin POSTs to SillyTavern's own server chat APIs (listing chats, reading chat file contents, saving chat files) using the host-provided request headers or a CSRF meta-tag fallback, and the responses are treated strictly as JSON chat message data for string matching. Nothing fetched is evaluated as code: there is no eval, no Function constructor, no script or style injection from remote data, and no external destination. The dynamic-looking sinks are a case-insensitive RegExp built from the user's own search term (with regex metacharacters escaped) and jQuery HTML updates whose interpolated values (file names, snippets, tags) pass through an escape helper first. Reading hidden/system messages and saving chat files with forced overwrite are disclosed behaviors required by the search, bookmark, theme, tag, and hide/unhide features described in the project documentation; persistence goes only to the user's own server-side extension settings and chat files. No attacker-controlled path to concrete harm is in,",
      "layman_explanation": "This is a chat search extension for SillyTavern. The flagged combination looks scary to an automated scanner because the code both talks to the network and constructs dynamic content, but nothing dangerous actually connects the two. The network traffic is just the extension reading and saving the user's own chat history on the user's own SillyTavern server, which is exactly what a search feature needs to do. No code is downloaded or executed, no data leaves the user's server, and the search highlight markup safely escapes text before displaying it.",
